Reason: After calling __sev_do_cmd_locked, the code unconditionally uses the firmware‑returned lengths (`data.cert_chain_len` and `data.pdh_cert_len`) to copy blob data to userspace. If the firmware command failed (e.g., due to an insufficient buffer), the firmware may set these lengths to values larger than the kernel‑allocated buffers. The subsequent `copy_to_user` calls then read out‑of‑bounds from the kernel buffers and leak sensitive data.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (ret || WARN_ON_ONCE(argp->error))
    goto e_free_cert;

Reason: This check is inserted after the lengths are reported to userspace (via `copy_to_user` of the input struct) but before the blob data is copied. If `__sev_do_cmd_locked` returned an error (`ret != 0`) or the firmware indicated an error (`argp->error` is non‑zero), the function jumps to the cleanup label `e_free_cert`, avoiding the out‑of‑bounds copies. The `WARN_ON_ONCE` also catches cases where the driver reports success but the firmware did not.

